<!--
  Imported automatically by every project in this tree, before the project file
  itself is read.

  It exists for one property. The target framework used to be repeated in four
  project files, which meant that moving it was four edits that could disagree
  with each other, and that a build for a different framework was not something
  you could ask for at all.

  net10.0 is the default and what the release packages are built against. Pass
  -p:ConnectorTargetFramework=net9.0 to build for .NET 9, which is what Visual
  Studio 2022 can open: VS 2022 has no .NET 10 support, so a net10.0 project
  will not load there whatever SDK is installed.

  The net9.0 line has its own branch, release/net9, where the default below is
  flipped and a global.json pins the SDK — one line of difference, so the two
  lines cannot drift apart in anything but that one decision. CI builds and
  tests net9.0 from main as well, so a change here cannot quietly break it.

  Package versions are NOT here. They are in Directory.Packages.props beside
  this file, which the SDK imports for the same tree and for the same reason:
  one decision, made once, rather than the same decision repeated in eleven
  project files. Nothing in this file needs to change when a package moves.
-->
<Project>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <ConnectorTargetFramework Condition="'$(ConnectorTargetFramework)' == ''">net10.0</ConnectorTargetFramework>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
